In .NET MAUI, the `Application` class no longer has `OnStart`, `OnSleep`, and `OnResume` virtual methods. Instead, use the **lifecycle events** on the `Window` class:

```csharp
public partial class App : Application
{
    protected override Window CreateWindow(IActivationState? activationState)
    {
        var window = new Window(new MainPage());

        window.Created += (s, e) => { /* replaces OnStart */ };
        window.Stopped += (s, e) => { /* replaces OnSleep */ };
        window.Resumed += (s, e) => { /* replaces OnResume */ };

        return window;
    }
}
```

**Key mappings:**
| Xamarin.Forms | .NET MAUI (Window event) |
|---------------|--------------------------|
| `OnStart()`   | `Window.Created`          |
| `OnSleep()`   | `Window.Stopped`          |
| `OnResume()`  | `Window.Resumed`          |

There are also more granular events like `Activated`, `Deactivated`, `Backgrounding`, and `Destroying` if you need finer control. You can also hook into platform-specific lifecycle events via `ConfigureLifecycleEvents()` in your `MauiProgram.cs`.
